DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       These functions are nonstandard; in new programs, the use of wwaaiittppiidd(2)
       or wwaaiittiidd(2) is preferable.

       The wwaaiitt33() and wwaaiitt44() system calls are similar to wwaaiittppiidd(2), but ad‐
       ditionally  return  resource  usage  information about the child in the
       structure pointed to by _r_u_s_a_g_e.

       Other than the use of the _r_u_s_a_g_e argument, the following wwaaiitt33() call:

           wait3(wstatus, options, rusage);

       is equivalent to:

           waitpid(-1, wstatus, options);

       Similarly, the following wwaaiitt44() call:

           wait4(pid, wstatus, options, rusage);

       is equivalent to:

           waitpid(pid, wstatus, options);

       In other words, wwaaiitt33() waits of any child, while wwaaiitt44() can  be  used
       to select a specific child, or children, on which to wait.  See wwaaiitt(2)
       for further details.

       If  _r_u_s_a_g_e  is  not  NULL, the _s_t_r_u_c_t _r_u_s_a_g_e to which it points will be
       filled with accounting information about the child.   See  ggeettrruussaaggee(2)
       for details.

